\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Predicting Formal Protest Petitions Against Housing Development:\par 
Evidence from Austin, Texas\par 
\par 
Author: Daniel Hardesty Lewis\par 
Advisor: Dory Kornfeld\par 
Degree: Master of Science in Urban Planning\par 
Institution: Graduate School of Architecture, Planning and Preservation, Columbia University\par 
Year: 2026\par 
\par 
---\par 
\par 
ABSTRACT\par 
\par 
This thesis investigates whether formal protest petitions against proposed housing\par 
developments in Austin, Texas can be anticipated before public hearings, using only\par 
the administrative and neighborhood records available at the time of application filing.\par 
The central question is whether parcel characteristics, neighborhood demographics, and\par 
application features observable by planning staff at filing can meaningfully identify\par 
which cases are likely to attract organized opposition.\par 
\par 
Using discretionary zoning cases from Austin's pre-HB 24 petition regime (2007-2024),\par 
predictive models are trained and evaluated on a temporal holdout that mimics forward\par 
administrative use. The results are qualified: filing-date features can rank petition\par 
risk above chance -- the best model achieves moderate out-of-distribution discrimination\par 
with a wide uncertainty interval (PR-AUC = 0.718, 95% CI: [0.48, 0.91]) -- but the\par 
rarity of petitioned cases and the model's limited external validity mean predictions\par 
are best used as relative triage rankings, not as case-level probabilities for\par 
high-stakes decisions.\par 
\par 
The core finding is that administrative data at filing carries real predictive signal\par 
for petition risk, yet that signal is too uncertain and context-dependent to support\par 
uncritical case-level decisions. The broader contribution is twofold: first, a\par 
methodology -- time-stamped features, temporal holdout evaluation, and explainability\par 
audits -- that is portable to other discretionary review systems; and second, the\par 
identification of neighborhood agency as a predictive bound. The study finds that the\par 
volatility of performance across petition thresholds serves as empirical evidence that\par 
mobilization is a strategic social choice that cannot be reduced to mechanistic\par 
administrative records.\par 
